WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.540
We’ve all had daydreams about being able
to fly. We can’t defy the laws of physics,

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:14.639
but we can still dream about it. So what does
it mean when a character in a film gets to

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:16.910
fly? Let’s look at a classic example.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:22.060
In E.T., the adults of the community, showing
conformity, hostility to anything new, xenophobia,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.480
suspicion, and a lack of imagination, are
bearing down on our young heroes. They’ve

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.000
even set up a roadblock. At just the moment
when things look worst, the bicycles leave

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.329
the earth and, with it, the earthbound grown-ups.
We see escape, wonder, magic, and freedom

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:34.780
through flight.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:35.420
Flying is freedom.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.480
Let’s do some closer readings of flying
in film

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.250
Jenny in Forrest Gump wants to be free. She
wants to fly away from her troubles. She has

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.770
a sexually abusive, alcoholic father. She
prays to fly away from him

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.300
But does she get her wings? Kind of. The police do take her to her grandmothers so she’s technically

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:59.079
free from her father, but this camera shot
tells a different story. The camera moves

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:03.739
up into the sky, and birds fly away with us,
but Jenny stays put on the ground. In a literal

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.470
sense, she does get freed from her father,
but figuratively, she’s still stuck. She

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:11.010
can’t fly away from the emotional damage
her father caused. Look at this shot of her

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.290
as an adult, still falling to the ground at
the sight of her old house. The camera shot

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.030
is placed firmly at ground level, reflecting
that she’s still grounded. She failed to

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.530
free herself from the shackles of her past.
Throughout the movie, we see her trying to

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:28.310
fly away and fail each time. The close up
on her shoes shows Jenny trying to leap out

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:32.900
of them, but she can’t. She’s stuck.
Later, she tries using drugs to forget her

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.100
abuse and fly away.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.100
The song "Free Bird" playing in the background during this scene is definitely no coincidence.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.380
Now when she wants to
fly, she’s higher up in a building, and

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.110
wearing high heels, but no matter how much
she tries extending herself upwards, she still

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:48.970
slips and faces the reality that she can’t
fly. She can’t escape the scars of her childhood.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.320
Only when Jenny goes back home to Forrest
does she start to emotionally recover. Nobody

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.450
can fly away from their past, but we can confront
it, accept it, and move on. She dies, but

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.070
she doesn’t die alone or trapped. She dies
in the company of people she loves, and that’s

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.830
when she truly becomes free, like a bird.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.280
Sometimes in film, the character does feel

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.670
like they’re flying. We can see parallels
between Jenny’s attempted flight and Rose’s

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:18.110
flight in Titanic. In the beginning, suicide
off the back of the Titanic seems to be the

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.500
only chance of escape Rose has from her rich,
plain lifestyle and unwanted marriage. With

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.010
Jack, however, she can now embrace the newfound
beauty in her life at the front of the boat.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.980
Look at the difference between these static,
dark close-ups on the back of the ship and

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.500
the sweeping shots of colors in the later
scene. We perceive some innate beauty to the

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.840
idea of flying that’s reflected in Titanic.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.660
Birdman has such an interesting portrayal

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.780
of flight because in the film, our hero, Riggan
Thomson, rejects flight. He chooses to stay

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.710
on the ground and perform his play. In Birdman,
flight is still a symbol of freedom, but it’s

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.790
a freedom Riggan wants to avoid. Riggan wants
to stay grounded and face reality. His play

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.640
seeks to comment on the issues of being human.
With flight, a person escapes the struggles

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.860
of being human, so flight means we no longer
need the philosophical discussions on being

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.730
human, because we’ve escaped the need for
them.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.290
Riggan wants to stay on the ground because
he wants to make art that actually means something.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:09.650
If he flies and makes superficial superhero
movies, he’ll be unable to make any profound

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:13.700
commentary on the human condition because,
through flight, he has escaped the human condition.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.530
He stays on the ground so he can comment on
the struggles of being grounded in his play.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.520
So what about the flight at the end of the
film? I think this is a different kind of

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.300
flight, mainly because it’s not just him
that sees it. His daughter does. By this time

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.860
in the film, Riggan has overcome the need
to please others. He does his play and does

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.490
what he wants purely for himself. He transcended
our need as humans to fit in and please others,

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.860
and we see this transcendence represented
through flight. He achieved a higher sense

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.420
of being, both literally 
and symbolically.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.960
So maybe we can't defy the laws of physics and fly.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.300
But if you manage to figure out yourself and your place in the universe, it sure feels like it.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:04.680
Thanks for watching.

